not changed unexpectedly. For workflows spanning multiple systems, I would generally favor saga-style coordination
over attempting a distributed transaction across every dependency.
Trade-off: Stronger consistency improves correctness but can reduce availability and
throughput.
50. How would you design disaster recovery for an
agentic platform?Answer: I would identify the critical durable assets: workflow state, approval records, configuration versions, audit evidence, and persistent knowledge. I would define recovery point and recovery time objectives based on business requirements. The platform should support tested database recovery, infrastructure reconstruction, secret restoration, and controlled restart of unfinished workflows. During regional failover, I would prevent duplicate execution of externally committed operations. I would run disaster-recovery exercises that simulate database unavailability, model-provider outages, and regional failures.
Principal-level insight: Restoring infrastructure is not sufficient; the platform must also restore
correct workflow execution semantics.
Section 6 — Security, identity, and AI
governance
51. How would you design zero-trust security for an
agentic AI platform?
Answer:
I would assume that no agent, tool, workload, or network segment is inherently trusted. Every request must have an authenticated identity, explicit authorization, and a bounded execution context. I would implement workload identity for services, user identity propagation for delegated actions, mTLS where appropriate, short-lived credentials, and policy enforcement at sensitive boundaries. Agent prompts and tool outputs would be treated as untrusted inputs. I would also separate the permissions required for reading data, generating recommendations, and performing mutations.
Principal-level insight: Zero trust requires continuous enforcement, not simply authentication
when a session starts.52. How do you implement least privilege and least agency?
Answer:
Least privilege limits what resources and operations an identity can access. Least agency extends this principle to the autonomy granted to an AI system. For example, an agent may have permission to create Jira issues but should not automatically be allowed to create unlimited issues without user confirmation or rate limits. I would define autonomy tiers: read-only assistance, recommendation generation, approval-required actions, and bounded autonomous execution. Each tier would have
