1.​ Token counting: Count system instructions, conversation history, retrieved chunks, tool responses, and reserved output before invoking the model. 2.​ Context compaction: Summarize older conversations when approaching the operational cap. 3.​ RAG selection: Retrieve and rerank evidence instead of placing entire documents into context. 4.​ Tool output truncation: Limit verbose tool responses and retain references to full results. 5.​ Durable memory: Store workflow state in PostgreSQL or another durable store, rather than relying on the context window.
6.​ Observability: Monitor token utilization, context truncation, latency, cost, and answer quality.
Modern models can support much larger windows. For example, OpenAI's current API model catalog lists models with approximately 1.05 million tokens, and Anthropic documents 1-million-token windows for supported Claude models.
