2. Step-by-step approach to deploying an AI platform
1.​ Define deployment requirements and SLOs​ Establish expected requests per second, peak concurrency, tenant count, model token usage, tool latency, availability, recovery targets, data residency, and cost limits. Separate interactive workloads from long-running jobs. 2.​ Choose the deployment topology​ Start with modular services deployed on ECS Fargate or Kubernetes. Use independent services where scaling, security boundaries, or release cycles justify them. Avoid unnecessary microservices. 3.​ Package and version components​ Build immutable container images for APIs, orchestrators, and agent workers. Version agent manifests, prompts, model policies, tool contracts, and evaluation datasets independently. 4.​ Provision infrastructure using IaC​ Use Terraform to provision networking, load balancers, compute, databases, queues, secrets, IAM roles, monitoring, and deployment permissions. Prefer private networking and short-lived workload credentials. 5.​ Deploy durable state and tool integrations​ Persist workflow checkpoints, idempotency records, and session references. Introduce a durable queue for asynchronous work. Route sensitive tools through policy enforcement and identity propagation. 6.​ Implement CI/CD and evaluation gates​ Run unit tests, contract tests, security scans, integration tests, agent golden-dataset evaluations, and performance tests. Block promotion when required quality or safety thresholds fail. 7.​ Release progressively​ Deploy to development, staging, and production. Use shadow evaluation where appropriate, then canary traffic such as 1% → 5% → 25% → 100%, provided metrics remain healthy.
8.​ Operate, observe, and recover​ Monitor p95 latency, token cost, model errors, tool failures, task completion, authorization denials, and queue backlog. Roll back versions or disable affected agents through feature flags.
3. Top 20 deployment interview
questions and answers Architecture and infrastructure Q1. How would you deploy an enterprise agentic AI platform?
 AI Evaluation | Principal Engineer Study Guide 72

I would separate the control plane from the execution plane. The control plane manages agent definitions, policies, model configurations, and release versions. The execution plane runs stateless orchestrators, specialist agents, model calls, and governed tools. I would deploy these through containers on ECS or Kubernetes, backed by durable state, queues, observability, and automated evaluation gates.
Q2. When would you choose AWS ECS Fargate over Kubernetes?
I would choose ECS Fargate for a relatively straightforward AWS-native platform where operational simplicity and managed infrastructure are priorities. I would choose Kubernetes when I need more complex scheduling, portability, custom operators, specialized inference workloads, or granular control over autoscaling and workload placement.
Q3. Should each AI agent be deployed as a separate microservice?
Not necessarily. I would initially deploy related agents within a shared runtime if they have similar scaling, permissions, and failure characteristics. I would separate agents when they require independent scaling, security isolation, deployments, or resource allocation.
Q4. How do you scale an AI platform horizontally?
Keep API and orchestration workers stateless, externalize workflow state, and scale replicas based on concurrency, queue depth, latency, and resource utilization. I would separately manage model-provider quotas and tool backpressure because adding application replicas does not increase downstream model capacity.
Q5. How do you manage control-plane and execution-plane availability?
The execution plane should use a last-known-good, versioned configuration snapshot when the control plane becomes temporarily unavailable. New configuration changes may pause, but existing authorized workflows should continue only while their policies remain valid and can still be enforced.
CI/CD, testing, and rollout Q6. How is CI/CD for AI platforms different from traditional software?
Traditional CI/CD validates code correctness and infrastructure. AI CI/CD must additionally evaluate model behavior, agent routing, groundedness, tool selection, safety, and regression against golden datasets. Model, prompt, and policy changes can introduce regressions even without code changes.
Q7. What is an evaluation harness as a release gate?
An evaluation harness runs predefined agent test cases against a candidate version and compares results against acceptance thresholds. For example, it can measure intent-routing accuracy, tool-call correctness, task completion, policy violations, and response quality. Promotion is blocked if mandatory thresholds fail.
Q8. How would you implement canary deployment for an AI agent?
I would deploy the new agent version alongside the stable version, route a small percentage of eligible traffic to it, and compare task success, latency, cost, tool errors, and policy violations. I would increase traffic only when quality and operational thresholds are satisfied.
Q9. How would you roll back a model or prompt version?
I would keep model and prompt configurations versioned, deploy them through a controlled release mechanism, and switch traffic back to the last-known-good configuration. I would also verify schema compatibility, in-flight workflow handling, and whether any side effects require reconciliation.
 AI Evaluation | Principal Engineer Study Guide 73

Q10. How do you handle database migrations during deployment?
I would use backward-compatible expand-and-contract migrations. First add new schema structures, deploy applications compatible with old and new formats, migrate data, and remove obsolete fields only after all dependent versions have been retired.
Reliability and distributed execution Q11. What happens if an agent worker crashes halfway through a workflow?
The workflow engine recovers from the last durable checkpoint. A new worker acquires the task using a lease or equivalent coordination mechanism, determines which steps completed, and resumes safely. External side effects require idempotency or reconciliation before retrying.
Q12. How do you implement idempotency in deployed agents?
I would generate a stable idempotency key for each logical business operation using identifiers such as tenant, workflow, step, and operation. I would persist execution status
with a unique constraint and use the same key across retries, preventing duplicate operations when delivery is at least once.
Q13. How do you manage partial failures?
I would classify errors as transient, permanent, or uncertain-outcome failures. Transient errors receive bounded retries with exponential backoff and jitter. Permanent failures terminate or request correction. Uncertain side effects are reconciled before any retry.
Q14. How do you prevent cascading failures when the model provider is slow?
Use timeouts, circuit breakers, concurrency limits, bounded queues, token budgets, and provider-specific rate limits. Where appropriate, degrade to cached results, smaller models, or asynchronous execution instead of allowing requests to accumulate indefinitely.
Q15. How do you achieve high availability?
Deploy application replicas across availability zones, use highly available managed data services, remove single-worker dependencies, and implement health checks and automated recovery. Define recovery objectives and test failure scenarios rather than assuming multi-AZ deployment alone guarantees availability.
Security, governance, and operations Q16. How do you secure an AI platform deployment?
I would use OAuth/JWT at the entry point, workload identity between services, least-privilege IAM, encrypted transport, centralized secrets management, tenant-scoped data access, and PDP/PEP authorization before tool execution. Model-generated instructions must never override security policies.
Q17. How do you manage secrets?
Secrets should live in a managed secret store, not in source code or container images. Services retrieve them using workload identity, with rotation, scoped access, and audit logging.
Q18. What observability is required?
I would instrument the complete request path with OpenTelemetry traces, correlation IDs, structured logs, and metrics. I would track request latency, agent steps, model tokens, tool calls, workflow outcomes, policy denials, and cost. Sensitive data should be redacted from telemetry.
 AI Evaluation | Principal Engineer Study Guide 74

Q19. How do you control AI platform costs?
I would apply per-tenant and per-workflow token budgets, route simple tasks to deterministic logic or smaller models, use validated caching, limit agent loops, and enforce concurrency quotas. Cost per successful task is more meaningful than token cost alone.
Q20. How would you handle a production incident caused by an incorrect AI action?
First contain the impact by disabling the affected agent, tool, or release through a kill switch. Then inspect traces, policy decisions, input evidence, and tool execution records. Reconcile or reverse actions where possible, add regression tests, and require successful evaluation before re-enabling the capability.
